+# Optional Unblock resource issuer
+
+This is source preparation for an isolated Unblock authorization branch. It is disabled by default. No production issuer deployment, real GCS transaction acceptance or actual ChatGPT web connection is established by this change.
+
+The dashboard and personal forks continue to use the Fulcra webapp skill's supplied authentication. They need no newly registered OAuth client or external database. This optional branch reuses this service's existing upstream Fulcra configuration and registered `/callback`, and offers dynamic registration for its OAuth clients.
+
+## Scope and binding
+
+The candidate issuer identifier is the existing service origin plus `/unblock`. Its metadata is at `/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/unblock`; its authorize, token, registration and revocation endpoints live under `/unblock`. The original issuer, tools, callbacks and token files retain their existing path.
+
+Each human approves one verified owned `my.fulcra.ai` dashboard, agent ID and environment. The requesting client and callback are displayed. Worker tokens are the native dashboard's existing sealed grants, capped by the upstream session and one hour. Renewal consent lasts at most 24 hours. Use the same agent/environment on reconnect to recover existing questions and action evidence.
+
+Only the `unblock:worker` scope is available. The client receives neither owner access nor owner refresh credentials. Existing Fulcra MCP tokens cannot authorize Unblock, and Unblock tokens do not enter this server's original MCP access-token namespace.
+
+## Durable state
+
+The existing configured `state_bucket` is accessed through the GCS API under `unblock/v1/`. Code, login, consent, owner, refresh, access and revocation keys are distinct and use hashed identifiers. Mounted-file writes and process locks are not used as cross-instance transactions. Missing configuration disables only this optional branch; unavailable source state is not an empty queue.
+
+Login state is independent of client state. Owner credentials belong to one verified login, rather than an OAuth client slot. A conditional claim precedes upstream refresh; an uncertain refresh is never repeated after restart. Known new credentials survive concurrent revocation for cleanup, while new access remains denied.
+
+An enrollment or revocation intent is retained before its external effect. Lost responses require exact native source readback. A retained renewal intent can reconcile its exact native receipt after replacement, using already-known owner access without taking over upstream refresh. Unknown receipts remain unresolved. Known refresh receipts can complete private publication without another grant; the old refresh token may recover the same replacement for 120 seconds, with the actual remaining access expiry. Code replay with matching client, redirect, PKCE and resource requests family revocation, including after code expiry. Public clients can revoke with their client ID and token; confidential clients retain the SDK authentication checks. Revocation does not return success while native readback is unresolved.
+
+## Before enabling a deployment
+
+Prepare an approved narrow rollout on the existing registered callback origin, or use a staging callback origin that is already authorized. Reuse the service's existing `oidc_server_url`, `oidc_client_id`, `fulcra_oidc_domain`, `fulcra_api` and `state_bucket`. The upstream callback stays `/callback`; a new staging hostname is not assumed to be registered. `UNBLOCK_ENABLED=true` also requires an explicit resource policy: `UNBLOCK_RESOURCES` is a JSON list of selected pilot resource URLs. For the later public rollout, `UNBLOCK_ALLOW_OWNED_RESOURCES=true` permits all canonically verified owned dashboards, so personal forks need no per-user central allowlist. Default policy is closed. This change sets no production flags, IAM permissions, client registrations or bucket lifecycle policies.
+
+Run an actual disposable-object generation/CAS probe with two clients in an explicitly authorized test bucket. Then prove real upstream login, consent, two-instance callback/code exchange, refresh uncertainty, project removal/domain change, native revocation and replay denial in both directions. The live native bridge proof is separate from issuer acceptance; unit generation fakes are separate from real GCS.
+
+Do not point an Unblock dashboard's protected-resource metadata at this issuer until its actual flow passes. Then prove ChatGPT web discovery, reconnect/expiry/revocation and replacement recovery. Event subscription and actual delayed agent pickup are subsequent work; these routes install no listener or persistent scheduler. Public plugin publication and publisher/license decisions remain separate.
+
+## Pending recovery
+
+Records remain private in the authorized bucket. A pending or uncertain record never establishes successful token issuance, revocation or agent progress. `UnblockProvider.recover_pending_code(code_key)` is a private operator method for a retained code record whose `revocation_requested` flag is set. It reads its frozen native intent/receipt and records actual revocation readback; it exposes no public recovery endpoint. Pending revocation uses its frozen grant ID even when re-enrollment correctly rejects that revoked grant.
+
+An operator must retain unresolved keys and poll/read them from the service environment until known, or surface the exact source/owner-reconnect prerequisite. There is no automatic lease takeover for unknown upstream credential effects. A disabled/replaced instance must not discard the bucket, private credentials, grant lineage or pending obligations. Establish that operational receiver before claiming unattended production coverage.
+
+Local tests cover the HTTP lifecycle with synthetic external authentication and generation-aware storage doubles. The original service's pre-existing skips, expected failures and Starlette warning remain reported separately. The implementation plan and broader host acceptance live in [ashfulcra/unblock-any-agent](https://github.com/ashfulcra/unblock-any-agent).
+
+The consent cookie uses Secure, HttpOnly, `__Host-`, Path=/ and SameSite=Lax for the top-level cross-site sign-in return. Explicit POST Origin, per-transaction cookie and nonce checks protect approval. A controlled real-browser callback/redirect probe omitted the Strict cookie and included the Lax cookie; this is separate from actual Fulcra login acceptance. [Browser cookie behavior](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Set-Cookie).
